          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 26. \u0938\u0902\u092f\u092e\u0903\u2014restraint over the mind (i. e. checking such\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"a desire ). \u0915\u093e\u0932\u0947\u2014at the proper time, timely ( charity ). \u0936\u0915\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092f\u093e\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"i. e. \u092f\u0925\u093e\u0936\u0915\u094d\u0924\u093f according to one's power or means. \u092f\u0941\u0935\u0924\u093f\u0966\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"maintaining silence with regard to speaking about other\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"peoples\u2019 wives; in such cases \u091c\u0928 means \u2018a collection.\u2019 \u092a\u0930\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0902\u2014is.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"connected with \u092f\u0941\u0935\u0924\u093f\u0966 by \u0938\u093e\u092a\u0947\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947\u093d\u092a\u093f \u0917\u092e\u0915\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d ( when the connec-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"tion is easily understood ) \u0938\u092e\u093e\u0938\u0903 \u0964 For a similar idea cf.,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"\u0905\u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0923\u0928\u0940\u092f\u0902 \u092a\u0930\u0915\u0932\u0924\u094d\u0930\u0902 \u0928\u093e\u092e \u0964 S'ak. V. \u092f\u0941\u0935\u0924\u093f is the fem. of \u092f\u0941\u0935\u0928\u094d, P\u00e1n.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"IV. 1. 77. \u0924\u0943\u0937\u094d\u0923\u093e\u0938\u094d\u0930\u094b\u0924\u0903\u2014The idea recurs in Vai. S'a. sl. 45. ( \u0906\u0936\u093e\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"\u0928\u093e\u092e \u0928\u0926\u0940 &c. ). \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0903\u2014\u0938\u0939 \u092e\u093e\u0928\u0947\u0928 \u0935\u0930\u094d\u0924\u0924\u0947 \u0938\u092e\u093e\u0928\u0903 \u0964 \u0924\u0924\u0903 \u0938\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0947 \u0937\u094d\u092f\u091e\u094d \u0964 See\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"V\u00e2rt. on P\u00e1n. V. 1. 124. Common to all the S'\u00e1stras. \u0905\u0928\u0941\u092a\u0939\u0924\u0966\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":13,\"text\":\"whose application (operation) is unobstructed i. e. which never\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":14,\"text\":\"fails to produce the desired effect. \u2018Which violates no rules or\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":15,\"text\":\"ordinances.\u2019 K. T. Telang. \u0936\u094d\u0930\u0947\u092f\u0938\u093e\u0902\u2014Blessings of all kinds.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit words and diacritics are difficult to distinguish; line 8 is a detached mark from line 9.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 27. \u0928\u0940\u091a\u0948\u0903\u2014by low or poor-spirited men, men incapable of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"sustained exertion. \u0928\u093f\u0915\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u092e\u094d \u0908\u0902 \u0936\u094b\u092d\u093e\u0902 \u091a\u093f\u0928\u094b\u0924\u0940\u0924\u093f; \u0928\u093f+\u0908+\u091a\u093f+\u0921\u0903 \u0964\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u092e\u0927\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0903\u2014men of the 2nd class. Cf. Mudr\u00e1r\u00e1kshasa Act. II. 70.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit derivation and the diacritics in the title are somewhat unclear.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 28. \u092a\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u2014Agreeable and yet not departing from, stand-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"ing to, justice, just. \u0935\u0943\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0903\u2014course of conduct, behaviour. \u092e\u0932\u093f\u0928\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u092e\u0932\u094b\u093d\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0938\u094d\u0924\u093f \u0964 \u092e\u0932\u094d + \u0907\u0928\u091a\u094d \u0905\u0938\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0947 \u0964 see P\u00e2n. V. 2. 114. A dirty or\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"foul deed. \u0905\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0903 &c.\u2014Because \u0905\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u0926\u093f\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0932\u093e\u092d\u0947\u093d\u092a\u093f \u0928 \u0917\u0924\u093f\u0930\u094d\u091c\u093e\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0936\u0941\u092d\u093e \u0964\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"\u0938\u0941\u0939\u0943\u0926\u094d\u2014For deri. see supra. 21. Mr. Joglekar takes this in the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"general sense of \u2018a noble minded person,\u2019 deriving it as \u0936\u094b\u092d\u0928\u0902\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"\u0939\u0943\u0924\u094d \u092f\u0938\u094d\u092f, as opposed to \u0905\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0903. But I am inclined to take it\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"rather in its restricted sense of \u2018a friend\u2019 who alone may be\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"appealed to for help under any circumstances, and not every\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"good person. The force of \u0905\u092a\u093f is then properly brought out.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"The use of the sing. \u0938\u0941\u0939\u0943\u0926\u094d also shows that that is the intention of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"the poet. Otherwise he should have used the plural correspond-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":13,\"text\":\"ing to \u0938\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0903. \u0909\u091a\u094d\u091a\u0948\u0903 \u0938\u094d\u0925\u0947\u092f\u092e\u094d\u2014Acting nobly, keeping up one's dignity.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":14,\"text\":\"Cf. S'is. IX. 5. \u092a\u0926\u092e\u0928\u0941\u0935\u093f\u0927\u0947\u092f\u0902 &c.\u2014Tread, follow, in the foot-steps\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":15,\"text\":\"( of the great ). \u0909\u0926\u094d\u0926\u093f\u0937\u094d\u091f\u2014is here used for \u0909\u092a\u0926\u093f\u0937\u094d\u091f advised, dictated.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":16,\"text\":\"\u0905\u0938\u093f\u0927\u093e\u0930\u093e\u0935\u094d\u0930\u0924\u092e\u094d\u2014Three meanings can be assigned to this:\u2014( 1 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some of the Sanskrit grammatical and quoted text is difficult to read.\"},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 14 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"26\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"27\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"28\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r4\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]},{\"flow\":\"notes\",\"units\":[]}],\"relations\":[]}"
